  "segments": [
    {
      "text": "You know, cooking dinner really depends on my mood. When I'm tired, usually by the end of the week, I just open the fridge and see what's left. Leftovers become my dinner, and that's fine with me. If I don't want to think too much, I keep it simple: eggs, bacon, and toast, easy, quick, and done. Most of the time, I enjoy cooking, but only if I've planned my meals. When I don't plan, it feels stressful. I tried improvising for a whole week once. Never again. Growing up, it was rare for my family to sit together for dinner. We only did it on special days like Christmas or New Year's Eve. I never really knew why. But now that I'm older, whenever a family member visits, I try to make sure we all sit down and share dinner at the same table. It feels simple but really meaningful. So yeah, cooking dinner changes with time. Sometimes it's about getting something on the plate, and other times, it's about making a moment to share with the people you love.",
      "start_time": 0.099,
      "end_time": 58.799,
